<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Chaco Canyon</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 900–1150</span> · <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a> · Also known as Chaco Culture National Historical Park</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Chaco Canyon is a canyon cut by the Chaco Wash in north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-mexico">New Mexico</a>, which between 900 and 1150 was a major centre of the Ancestral Puebloans. Its builders raised 15 major complexes of quarried sandstone and hauled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">timber</a>, which remained the largest buildings in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-america">North America</a> until the 19th century, and largely straight roads linked it with outlying communities. The Chacoan system began to unravel around 1140, perhaps triggered by a 50-year drought that began in 1130. A national monument from 1907, it has been a World Heritage Site since 1987.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/chaco-canyon.jpg" alt="Chaco Canyon" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: National Park Service (United States) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Chaco_Canyon_Chetro_Ketl_great_kiva_plaza_NPS.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
